 "text": " Inside the CPU and RAM, there are no words or pictures, only bits, tiny switches that are either off or on. Group 8 bits together and you get 1 byte, which can represent a number from 0 to 255. Using basic logic gates like N, which outputs 1 only when both inputs are 1, or N0, the CPU combines billions of bit switches to do math. Let's see how the CPU actually runs the program using our interactive Fetch, Decode, Execute simulator in the study guide. Watch the program counter on the left. It holds the memory address of the next instruction. First, when I click Fetch, the CPU reads the instruction from that RAM address and loads it into the instruction register. Second, when I click Decode, the control unit figures out what that instruction means, such as load 14 or add 28. an increments the program counter so it is ready for the next line third when I click execute the arithmetic logic unit performs the calculation and stores the result in the accumulator updating the total from 14 to 42 your computer repeat this exact fetch decode execute heartbeat billions of times every second",
